! Peer Coordination — Automerge Rendezvous Doctrine

!! The Yjs / Automerge Difference

In Yjs, room name = doc identity. Two peers calling `new Y.Doc()` with the same
provider room string rendezvous automatically — the provider maps name → CRDT state.

In `@automerge/automerge-repo`, a random UUID (`AutomergeUrl`) carries doc identity.
Two `repo.create()` calls always produce two ''separate, non-merging documents''.
`Repo.import(binary)` does NOT produce a deterministic URL from content — it
generates a new random UUID per import. The library ships no native content-addressed
rendezvous primitive.

''Corollary:'' any system that lets two peers independently create "the same wiki"
without sharing a URL produces two hermetic worlds that never merge.
Patching this at the reaction layer treats a coordination failure as an
application concern — wrong layer.

!! The UEFN Lesson: Author-Time Identity

UEFN/Verse handles island-level shared state through ''device identity assigned''
''at author-time (edit-time), not at boot-time''. The author places devices in the island
editor, each device receives a stable UUID from the editor, and the project checks those IDs in.
The island file SERVES AS the rendezvous — runtime code never calls "create new
device"; it calls "find device at this author-assigned ID."

''The lesson for predefined wikis (UNBUILT):'' the island author MUST assign each wiki docUrl in a
committed island definition before boot. Peers join that URL; they never create predefined wiki docs.
What the house built instead follows.

!! Canonical Architecture

''Oracle and board addresses derive from shared seeds; other shared bag URLs come from `repo.create()`''
''and travel as pointer records on the oracle or the catalog.''
`deterministicDocUrl(seed)` takes the first 16 bytes of `sha256(seed)` as the doc id
(`packages/lararium-mesh/src/deterministic-doc.ts`). Two families ride it:

* the ''oracle plane'' — `oracleGenesisDocUrl` derives its address from a well-known seed, so every
  vessel materializes the same doc under one stable id;
* the ''per-Nexus boards'' — crossroads, WHO, Kapae antigen, carriage, vouch, edge-kapae and
  persona-KEL derive from the Nexus key; each hosting doc derives from both the Nexus and a hearth key.
  Vessels given the same inputs resolve the same address.

```
seed (a well-known string, or a URI + the Nexus key)
        │  deterministicDocUrl
        ▼
All peers: materializeSharedLarDoc(repo, url)  ←  find first; else import the pinned blank under that id
        │
        ▼
one CRDT truth per board  ←  each vessel writes its own entries under its own actor
```

''The first-boot race converges by construction.'' `materializeSharedLarDoc` finds the doc when a prior
boot or a synced peer holds it. Otherwise the vessel imports a blank doc under the deterministic id, and
`pinnedDoc` pins both the actor and the clock of that blank (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/pinned-doc.ts`) —
so two vessels racing to stand first import byte-identical bytes, share one history, and merge.

''The rest mint at random.'' A vessel's private docs come from `repo.create()`, so no peer derives their
ids. The shared lares and lararium corpus docs mint the same way in the node's genesis office, which records
each URL on the oracle doc; two node homes federating one oracle may race that mint, LWW settles it, and
each re-reads and adopts the winner (`packages/lararium-node/src/genesis-artifact.ts`).

''UNBUILT: the committed island file.'' No `lararium-island.ts`, no `ISLAND.catalogDocUrl` and no
`ISLAND.wikis` table stand in source. Derivation carries the author-time rendezvous instead.

!! Dynamic Wiki Bootstrap Protocol

''UNBUILT.'' The design below covers a wiki that two peers may each create at runtime under one name. No
source implements it: no `contentDocUrl` field stands in code.

```
1. repo.find(catalogDocUrl) → catalogHandle
   (waitHandleLocal: serve from NodeFS/IndexedDB first; merge remote opportunistically)

2. canonicalUrl = catalogHandle.doc().wikis[wikiId]?.contentDocUrl

3a. if present → repo.find(canonicalUrl)  [fast path, no race]

3b. if absent →
      myHandle = repo.create({})
      catalogHandle.change(doc => {
        doc.wikis[wikiId] = { contentDocUrl: myHandle.url }
      })
      await tick  // let Automerge resolve concurrent writes by actor-ID + Lamport clock
      canonicalUrl = catalogHandle.doc().wikis[wikiId].contentDocUrl
      if canonicalUrl !== myHandle.url →
        // lost the CRDT race — migrate writes and discard orphan
        Automerge.merge(canonicalDoc, myHandle.doc())  // rebase local changes
        repo.find(canonicalUrl)                        // join canonical
      else →
        myHandle  // won the race
```

Automerge's LWW map resolves concurrent `wikis[wikiId]` writes deterministically
by actor-ID + Lamport timestamp. The losing peer migrates via `Automerge.merge()`
(valid because both docs share intended content), then discards its orphan URL.

!! Keyhive Path

Keyhive (Ink & Switch — Zelenka, Mumm, Good) builds Automerge-native access control.

* ''Doc IDs never serve as Ed25519 public keys.'' Some docs receive random IDs; the oracle and the
  per-Nexus boards derive theirs from stable seeds. Their URLs keep the `automerge:<base58-id>` form.
  Keyhive adds an access-control layer ON TOP — it does not change the URL scheme.
* ''Individuals'' have stable Ed25519 keypairs → DIDs. ''Documents'' have their own
  Keyhive group structure (separate from the individual's key).
* ''"Add member"'' commits a signed operation to the doc's Keyhive ACL log,
  referencing the new peer's Ed25519 public key. No separate invite token — the
  signed "add" entry SERVES AS the invitation artifact, delivered out-of-band then
  applied when the peer connects.
* ''Convergent Capabilities'' replace UCAN JWTs with CRDT-compatible capability
  tokens. Concurrent delegation and revocation resolve without coordination.
* ''Group Management CRDT'' handles wiki membership (who can write) as Automerge
  ops — self-certifying, no server vote required.
* ''Beelay'' (automerge/beelay) accompanies Keyhive as its sync protocol. It operates over
  ciphertext only (servers never see plaintext). Peers authenticate the connection
  and advertise which docs they hold capability for — the catalog problem dissolves
  into capability propagation.

''As built.'' The house pins `@keyhive/keyhive` at `0.3.0-alpha.1`, a pre-alpha, behind a narrow
`CapabilityProvider` (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/capability-provider.ts`). The node vessel's
`sharePolicy` runs a class-keyed verdict (`packages/lararium-node/src/self-slot-share.ts`), never the open
`async () => true` stub. `sharePolicy` stays ADVISORY: it decides which docs a repo volunteers, and a private
plane's random 16-byte id carries its confidentiality against a peer that cannot guess it
(`packages/lararium-mesh/src/federation-gate.ts`). No Beelay provider stands.

''UNBUILT: the Keyhive-gated wiki join.'' In that design ''only the island author creates wiki docs; every other peer''
''receives an "add member" signed op referencing its Ed25519 key, then joins''
''via `repo.find(url)`, with the url exchanged out-of-band (invitation link, DID''
''service endpoint, or social graph record)'', and a Keyhive capability check answers `sharePolicy`.
`KeyhiveIdentitySlot` stands specified in code and tested as that check, out of its package barrel; no
production call site constructs it, and both vessels fill the inner ring with the PersonaGroup ring
(`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-identity-slot.ts`).

!! Reaction Write-Back Invariants

A reaction runs per peer, and some reactions write back to the shared CRDT store. No `ReactionEngine`
class stands: dispatch lives in the `reaction-router` TW5 startup module over `ReactionGraph`
(`packages/lararium-tw5/src/modules/reaction-router.ts`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/reaction-graph.ts`).
Five invariants keep write-back safe. ''As a set they read as design law'': no wiki gate state, no
reaction-level idempotence check and no `authoritativeReactions` capability stand in code; the store does
skip a record whose content already stands (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/automerge-doc-store.ts`).

''1. Canonical URL confirmed before any write-back.''  
A peer must resolve a wiki's canonical address before accepting writes for it.
UNBUILT: wikis MUST gate on `BOOTSTRAPPING → RESOLVING → OPEN`, and write-back MUST run only
in `OPEN`.

''2. Output identities MUST derive from content (idempotent).''  
`store.put(record)` only. Never `store.append()`, never counter increments.
Two peers firing the same reaction on the same input must produce
identical `store.put()` calls — Automerge deduplicates naturally.
If output depends on peer-local state (timestamp, random ID), the reaction counts as
non-idempotent and must sit behind a capability gate (see #4).

''3. A write to an orphan docUrl re-routes or drops; it never lands.''  
After catalog resolution, a peer either re-routes any write targeted at a losing docUrl
to the canonical URL or drops it with a logged error. A write to an
orphan stays invisible to the canonical doc, and no peer may accept one silently.

''4. Non-idempotent reactions gated to the authoritative peer.''  
UNBUILT: a peer capability named `authoritativeReactions` MUST gate every non-idempotent reaction
(generate a summary tiddler, send a notification). The node peer would hold that capability and a browser
peer would not, so each such reaction fires exactly once — on the node peer — never N times across N
browser peers.

''5. Stable actorId per node, not per boot.''  
Automerge's LWW conflict resolution uses actor IDs + Lamport timestamps. If a
reaction writes as "the system," that actor must carry a persistent identity
derived from the node's keypair — never regenerated per boot. Unstable actor IDs
cause spurious conflicts and make LWW resolution non-deterministic across restarts.
`KeyhiveIdentitySlot.deriveActorId` derives a stable actor id from the identity's DID, and no production
call site invokes it.

!! Automerge-Repo: What the Official Docs Say

The Automerge team's canonical pattern for multi-peer root docs
(`automerge.org/docs/tutorial/persist-root-doc/`):

```ts
let rootDocUrl = localStorage.rootDocUrl
if (!rootDocUrl) {
  const handle = repo.create()
  localStorage.rootDocUrl = rootDocUrl = handle.url
}
const root = repo.find(rootDocUrl)
```

`repo.import(binary, docId)` can merge orphan docs but ''only if they share''
''history'' — the docs explicitly warn against assigning the same ID to documents
that never shared any history. So the library offers no native content-addressed
rendezvous, and the house supplies the shared history itself: for a per-Nexus board, every vessel
imports the same pinned blank under the derived id; for the oracle, every vessel imports bytes built
from the genesis seed under its derived id.

`automerge-repo-bundles` (Repo 2.0, May 2025) allows serializing multiple docs
with their IDs as a single import payload — relevant for shipping an initial
doc bundle to new nodes.

